---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Is criminology a good career in India? It is a good subject and a thin direct career: no police, prison or court cadre hires on a criminology degree alone.</strong>
    It pays when you pair it with data, law or psychology and aim at a named route such as fraud analysis, policy research or correctional work.
    Building that <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> before you pay for a costly master's is the decision that unlocks stronger income opportunities and moves you toward earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Criminology studies why crime happens, who is harmed and how justice systems respond. It is a social science, not a lab course.</li>
      <li>The openings that exist (police and CAPF exams, correctional posts, private fraud work) mostly accept other degrees too. Your edge is the second skill, not the label.</li>
      <li><strong>Pair it with data, law or psychology, build one public piece of analysis, and spend no more than about 10% of your education budget on the degree.</strong></li>
      <li>Skip it if you want detective glamour, a guaranteed salary, or lab work. Test the real daily work first.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    This page covers criminology as a social-science career. If your pull is the lab side (DNA, digital evidence, state labs), read
    <a href="/blog/career-options/is-forensic-science-a-good-career/">is forensic science a good career in India</a>
    instead. The two overlap, but their gates and pay are different.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="verdict">The verdict, route by route</h2>

  <p>
    Criminology is not one job, so the answer changes by route. The table starts with the routes that can grow past a fixed pay scale and ends with the ones that cannot. The first four need a skill you add on top of the degree.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Route</th>
          <th>Who pays, and how they hire</th>
          <th>What you need</th>
          <th>Scalability and why</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {routeRows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Route"><strong>{r.route}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Who pays, and how they hire">{r.pays}</td>
            <td data-label="What you need">{r.gate}</td>
            <td data-label="Scalability and why"><span class={`scale-chip scale-chip--${r.cls}`}>{r.label}</span><br />{r.why}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Sources: NCRB Crime in India 2023 as reported by PTI; NCRB Prison Statistics India 2022 as reported by LiveLaw; UPSC CAPF 2026 as reported by Careers360. Check the official notices for current figures.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The two best-known routes, police and CAPF exams, are the most crowded and the least tied to the degree. The growing money is in fraud, cyber and research work, and those employers ask for proof of skill first.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="what-it-is">Criminology is not forensic science</h2>

  <p>
    Many students pick criminology expecting crime scenes. That is a different field with a different gate.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Criminology (social science)</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Asks why crime happens, who is harmed and what prevention and punishment do.</li>
        <li>Tools: statistics, interviews, case law, field visits.</li>
        <li>Output: reports, policy notes, programme evaluations.</li>
        <li>No single recruitment gate. You build routes yourself.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Forensic science (lab)</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Examines physical or digital evidence for one case.</li>
        <li>Tools: lab methods, software, chain-of-custody paperwork.</li>
        <li>Output: court-ready lab reports.</li>
        <li>Government labs recruit through a separate exam.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Inside criminology, the programme at TISS lists these themes: criminology, child rights and juvenile justice, human rights and access to justice, criminal and correctional laws, and victimology and crime prevention. They fall into four branches.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid feature-grid--four">
    {fieldCards.map((c) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{c.label}</div>
        <div class="feature-card__title">{c.title}</div>
        <p>{c.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="degrees">Degrees and institutes, with entry rules</h2>

  <p>
    These are the programmes we could confirm from official 2026-27 notices, with a note where only a third-party listing was available. Fees and rules change, so confirm on the institute site before you apply.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Institute</th>
          <th>Programme</th>
          <th>Entry</th>
          <th>Cost</th>
          <th>Watch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {degreeRows.map((d) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Institute"><strong>{d.inst}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Programme">{d.prog}</td>
            <td data-label="Entry">{d.entry}</td>
            <td data-label="Cost">{d.cost}</td>
            <td data-label="Watch">{d.watch}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Sources: NFSU Programme Details for AY 2026-27; TNDALU admission notification 2026-27; Bar and Bench on RRU admissions 2026; TISS admissions page; Careers360 listing for Madras; Ambedkar University Delhi admission notices.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      <strong>Budget check.</strong> Treat roughly 10% of your total education budget as the starting line for college, and above about 20% as a caution that needs course-specific proof. On a Rs 10 lakh budget, the NFSU B.Sc. tuition (Rs 3.9 lakh) is 39% and the NFSU M.A. tuition (Rs 2.4 lakh) is 24%. TISS at Rs 1.64 lakh is about 16%. These are planning heuristics, not rules, and they leave out hostel and living costs.
    </p>
    <p>
      The free material is strong: NCRB Crime in India and Prison Statistics India are public, and so are the BPR&amp;D police data reports. Read them for a few weeks before paying for a master's. Judge each course on what its recent graduates actually do.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="gap">The gap between a degree and a job opening</h2>

  <p>
    No source we found counts openings that ask for a criminology degree, which tells you something. The jobs that do exist sit in cadres and companies that hire on other tests.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Police:</strong> BPR&amp;D data showed 5,31,737 vacant posts in state police forces on 1 January 2020. The gate is a general exam, not criminology.</li>
    <li><strong>CAPF:</strong> 349 Assistant Commandant posts in 2026, graduates aged 20 to 25, with a written test, physical test and interview.</li>
    <li><strong>Correctional staff:</strong> 820 in place against 1,468 sanctioned at the end of 2022, a 44% gap, while the Model Prison Manual (2016) benchmark of one per 200 prisoners would need about 2,866. States control hiring, so the need does not automatically become advertised posts.</li>
    <li><strong>Cyber and fraud:</strong> 86,420 cyber crime cases in 2023, up about 31%, with 59,526 of them fraud. Employers here want technical skill.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      <strong>Two stages, not one.</strong> Exam routes filter you at entry, again in training, and again at promotion. Teaching filters you at the NET and PhD, then again at a small number of permanent posts. Plan a second route in case the first one stalls.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="stack">Law, psychology or data: the pairing that gets hired</h2>

  <p>
    Use one core skill, one multiplier and one piece of proof. Criminology is the context. The multiplier is what an employer can test.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {stackCards.map((c) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{c.label}</div>
        <div class="feature-card__title">{c.title}</div>
        <p>{c.body}</p>
        <div class="card-subrow card-subrow--positive"><span class="card-subrow__label">Proof</span> {c.proof}</div>
        <div class="card-subrow"><span class={`scale-chip scale-chip--${c.cls}`}>{c.tag}</span></div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    Start with data if you are undecided. It opens the most routes and costs the least. Good YouTube lessons, reputable low-cost courses and public tables can teach it, but judge each course by the analysis you can show at the end, not the certificate. Law and psychology need formal degrees, so spend on them only once a route is clear. English writing matters on every route, because the product is a report someone else must trust. Together, the pairing and the public analysis are your high-value skill portfolio. Data and research work can also be done remotely, while correctional and police-linked work means being on site, so match the route to how you want to work.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pay">Pay: what can and cannot be said</h2>

  <p>
    There is no reliable salary survey for criminology graduates. The portal tables we found mix roles from different fields, include foreign jobs, and contradict themselves, so we do not repeat them.
  </p>

  <p>
    One fact has a named source: UPSC CAPF Assistant Commandants are Group A officers at Level 10 of the pay matrix, reported as Rs 56,100 to 1,77,500 per month. The sources we checked disagree on some details, so use the official notification. Private pay depends on the skill you add, the employer and the city.
  </p>

  <p class="note-line">
    Pay figures here are indicative ranges and averages from public profiles, not exact or guaranteed amounts. Pay differs by city, employer, skill and year, and markets change. Check current job listings (for example on LinkedIn, Naukri or Indeed) and ask people working in the role before you decide.
  </p>

  <h2 id="business">Who pays, and the business layers</h2>

  <p>
    Most criminology jobs are employer-only. The business layer matters for the side route, or for the move after a few years of experience.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>The scalable path:</strong> a small practice that sells analysis, training or research writing. It scales because you set the price per assignment, one tested session can become a package, and a public body of work brings clients without cold selling.
    </p>
  </div>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Consulting:</strong> fraud-pattern reviews and risk checks for small companies and law firms.</li>
    <li><strong>Training:</strong> cyber-fraud and safety sessions for schools, offices and housing societies.</li>
    <li><strong>Research writing:</strong> state crime-data briefs, a newsletter, and reports for NGOs and funders.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    How they find and trust you: through a sample analysis they can read, a referral from someone they know, and a clear one-line offer. The first sales skill worth learning is positioning. Pick one niche, such as UPI fraud for small shops, and write one sample report on it. Follow up after every talk. The one number to watch is how many of your short intro messages turn into a call.
  </p>

  <h2 id="detective">Private investigation: the rules in India</h2>

  <p>
    Many students ask about becoming a detective. The honest picture is a gap in the law.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>The Private Detective Agencies (Regulation) Bill, 2007 would have required licences. PRS India records it was withdrawn in the Rajya Sabha on 23 March 2020, so no licensing law exists.</li>
    <li>The Private Security Agencies (Regulation) Act, 2005 covers guarding agencies, not detective work.</li>
    <li>The 2017 Puttaswamy judgment recognises privacy as a fundamental right. Following people or taking their data without legal authority carries risk, and evidence gathered that way may not be accepted.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If the interest is investigation, the safer route is corporate due diligence and fraud checks inside a firm, with documents and public records, not surveillance.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI and crime analytics</h2>

  <p>
    AI is changing the analysis layer fastest. The honest split:
  </p>

